# Dataplatform CI/CD

This managed guide describes the minimum workflow for this dataplatform. See `CONTRIBUTING.md` for
development and review conventions.

## Branching

- `main` is protected and is the only long-lived branch.
- Use short-lived `feature/*`, `bump/hub-*`, `fix/*`, or `chore/*` branches.
- DEV, UAT, and PROD are dbt targets and GitHub Environments, not Git branches.
- Never edit installed hub-package files under `dbt_packages/`.

## Hub package updates

A `bump/hub-*` PR updates these items together:

1. the full 40-character hub commit SHA in `packages.yml`;
2. the resolved dbt package lock; and
3. all physical source bindings required by the new package.

The concrete mechanism for item 3:

```powershell
kairos-ontology bump-hub <ref>                    # 1: pins packages.yml to the new SHA
uv run dbt deps                                   # 2: refreshes the resolved package lock
kairos-ontology validate-source-bindings          # see which bindings need re-confirmation
# ...review the reported `stale` findings, and any `missing`/`unknown`/`duplicate` ones...
kairos-ontology validate-source-bindings --confirm  # 3: stamp bindings verified for the new SHA
```

`validate-source-bindings` reads the new pin from `packages.yml` automatically and reports a
`stale` finding for every physically bound source whose `meta.kairos.verified_hub_sha` (see
`models/_sources.yml`'s header comment) does not match it -- i.e. has not been explicitly
re-confirmed correct since the bump. `--confirm` only stamps once every other finding (missing,
unknown, duplicate) is already clean, so review and fix those first. Commit the stamped binding
file(s) as part of the same `bump/hub-*` PR, before opening it.

Do not pin a moving branch in production. A release tag may help reviewers find the hub release,
but the package revision is the commit SHA.

## Pull-request checks

`.github/workflows/pr-validate.yml` runs on every pull request against `main`, entirely on a
GitHub-hosted runner, with **no warehouse credentials required**:

```powershell
uv sync --locked
uv run dbt deps
uv run dbt parse --profiles-dir .dbt --target ci
uv run dbt compile --profiles-dir .dbt --target ci
uv run kairos-ontology validate-source-bindings
```

This schema-level default is deliberate: a freshly scaffolded dataplatform has no warehouse CI
credentials configured, so a literal full build by default would fail out of the box for every new
hub. The workflow writes its own throwaway, credential-free `ci` target (the same synthetic
`offline.invalid` placeholder the hub's own `validate-dbt` gate uses) so `dbt parse`/`dbt compile`
have a `--target ci` to resolve against without needing any secret. `validate-source-bindings` must
fail closed before any warehouse execution when a package source lacks a real physical binding, or
the binding is unknown, duplicate, or stale — and it needs no credentials either, since it only
compares package source usage against `models/_sources.yml`.

### Opt-in: `dbt build --empty` — the only gate that catches SQL-dialect errors

Add this before you consider a full build. It is cheap, and it closes a real gap that
nothing else in either repository can.

dbt does **not** abstract SQL dialects: a model body is handed to the engine verbatim,
so `dbt parse` and `dbt compile` never inspect it, and neither does any offline gate in
the ontology hub. `--empty` runs every model with `limit 0` — the warehouse parses,
binds and plans the SQL but reads no rows and writes nothing — which makes it the first
point in the whole pipeline where dialect errors surface, and it catches all of them,
including ones nobody wrote a rule for.

```powershell
uv run dbt build --empty --profiles-dir .dbt --target ci
```

It needs the same real, credential-isolated `ci` target the full build does (below), but
essentially no warehouse compute. Make it a required check on `bump/hub-*` pull requests:
that is the PR that adopts a new hub SHA, and therefore the first moment newly emitted SQL
could be wrong.

### Opt-in: full `dbt build`

Add a full, isolated `dbt build --profiles-dir .dbt --target ci` step only once this repository has
a real, credential-isolated `ci` target — add it as a GitHub Environment secret, never commit it to
`.dbt/profiles.yml`, and remove (or make conditional) the workflow's placeholder-profile step first,
since a build needs a `ci` target that can actually reach a warehouse:

```powershell
uv run dbt build --profiles-dir .dbt --target ci
```

The full build runs applicable unit tests, materializes models, and then runs data tests in
dependency order; introduce Slim CI only when project scale demonstrates that it is necessary.

## Promotion

After merge, every job checks out the same dataplatform commit:

```text
commit D8 -> DEV build -> approval -> UAT build -> approval -> PROD build
```

Use GitHub Environments for credentials and approvals. Retain `manifest.json`,
`run_results.json`, and the workflow run for each deployment. Never rebuild from current `main`
during an existing promotion.

## Rollback and PROD hotfix

Rollback redeploys the last known-good dataplatform SHA from GitHub deployment history.

For an urgent fix when `main` has advanced:

1. branch from the exact SHA running in PROD;
2. add the smallest fix and a regression test;
3. run required checks and deploy that exact hotfix SHA after approval;
4. create a new branch from current `main`;
5. cherry-pick or reapply the same fix; and
6. merge the forward-port through a normal PR.

Never merge the old production branch wholesale into current `main`.

## Fabric and Power BI

dbt deployment and Fabric/Power BI artifact deployment are separate lanes. The Power BI workflow
consumes `powerbi-semantic-model.zip` from the same hub release as the dbt package. The
archive contains the first generated `SemanticModel` and `Report` versions.

The workflow must accept and verify the expected hub SHA and archive SHA-256 before extraction.
After the target environment's dbt build succeeds, it uses that GitHub Environment's credentials
to deploy both `SemanticModel` and `Report` item types. DEV, UAT, and PROD receive the same archive;
only approved workspace and connection parameterization changes.

Power BI rollback redeploys the previously successful archive. Never regenerate or hand-edit TMDL,
PBIR, or report JSON in the dataplatform. Any wrapper generation or TMDL normalization happens in
the hub before the archive is validated and checksummed; downstream deployment is read-only over
item content.

### Direct Lake connection overrides

Deploy-time parameterization is the exception to "read-only", and is this repo's job by design.
A Fabric Direct Lake semantic model resolves every partition through one named expression whose
OneLake URL embeds a specific workspace and lakehouse. The hub bakes its own default environment's
URL into the TMDL and ships a `parameter.yml` so fabric-cicd can rewrite it per environment — but
the hub can only enumerate environments it declares in its own `kairos.yaml`, which would force
every Fabric workspace this platform ever deploys to into a repo that is otherwise
infrastructure-agnostic.

Instead, declare the workspace and lakehouse IDs this platform owns in
`.github/fabric/gold-connections.yml` (copy `gold-connections.yml.example`):

```yaml
environments:
  PROD:
    workspace_id: "${FABRIC_PROD_WORKSPACE_ID}"
    lakehouse_id: "${FABRIC_PROD_LAKEHOUSE_ID}"
```

A whole value written as `${VAR}` is resolved from the deploy environment, so real GUIDs need
never be committed. The deploy workflow applies this after the archive's SHA-256 has been
verified, via `kairos-ontology apply-gold-connection`, which:

- rewrites only `replace_value[<target_environment>]` in the extracted `parameter.yml`;
- leaves `find_value` exactly as the hub emitted it — fabric-cicd matches it as a literal
  substring against the URL in the TMDL, so a locally supplied value would silently fail to
  match and leave the model pointed at the hub's default workspace;
- never touches the `.zip`, its checksum, or any TMDL/PBIP file;
- logs the before/after URL into the run log;
- is a clean no-op when the file is absent or declares no matching environment, in which case
  the hub's own values stand.

## Updating this guide

Adopt a newer toolkit and managed guide in an isolated PR:

```powershell
uv run kairos-ontology update --upgrade
# On Windows, wait for the detached managed-file refresh to finish.
uv run kairos-ontology update --check
```

If the toolkit dependency is already current, run `uv run kairos-ontology update` without
`--upgrade`; plain `update` completes synchronously. Only a version-changing Windows
`update --upgrade` requires checking `.kairos/upgrade-refresh.log`. Review local environment,
source-binding, and workflow changes manually; the update command cannot infer them.

### One-time migration for a dataplatform scaffolded before this pin existed

`update --upgrade` refuses (rather than silently doing nothing) if `pyproject.toml` has no
`[tool.kairos] channel` block. Dataplatforms scaffolded before this existed pinned the toolkit
via an unversioned `[tool.uv.sources]` git dependency instead. Migrate once, by hand:

```toml
# Replace this:
dependencies = [
    "kairos-ontology-toolkit",
    ...
]
[tool.uv.sources]
kairos-ontology-toolkit = { git = "https://github.com/{ORG}/kairos-ontology-toolkit" }

# With this (pick a real released tag, e.g. from
# https://github.com/Cnext-eu/kairos-ontology-toolkit/releases):
dependencies = [
    "kairos-ontology-toolkit @ https://github.com/Cnext-eu/kairos-ontology-toolkit/releases/download/<tag>/kairos_ontology_toolkit-<version>-py3-none-any.whl",
    ...
]
[tool.kairos]
channel = "stable"  # or "preview" / an explicit tag, matching whatever <tag> above is
```

Then `uv lock && uv sync`. `update --upgrade` works normally from here on.
